    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d92">

                <pb facs="0205" n="99" sameAs="frus:frus1906p1/pg99"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Belgian Minister to the
                            Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington, D. C.</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1906-06-04"><hi rendition="#italic">June 4,
                        1906</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Secretary of State</hi>: I am glad to take
                    this opportunity of pointing out to your attention the fact that amongst the 15
                    members of the commission appointed by His Majesty to <pb facs="0206" n="100"
                        xml:id="pg100"/> devise the necessary reforms for the Kongo administration
                    only two may be considered as owning financial interests in some of the Kongo
                    companies. The bulk of the commission’s membership is made up of experienced
                    officials of the administration, both civil and military. Furthermore, the three
                    most important members of the commission are Chief Justice van Maldeghem, of the
                    supreme court of Belgium; Attorney-General Janssens, of the same court, who was
                    president of the commission of inquiry sent out to investigate the conditions in
                    the Kongo; and Associate Justice Nys, of the Brussels court of appeals, who is a
                    member of the permanent court of arbitration of The Hague.</p>
                <p>I beg to remain, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bn. Moncheur.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
